99027 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099027 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29beae2b-e3ad-54c1-b38c-77047fe5ae6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099028 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099028 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099028 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f07a31e-afd5-574c-b84a-4c775953dcd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099029 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c282323-a32d-5969-83ed-d938a4ad4bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099030 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f9ab6ce-37b7-526f-92ce-0279e52b7b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3e/3e9a879522107eb2b2381a688d1173fdf6a93a8b81686fd2f21c815008ec35df.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10$. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caa282bb-e637-522c-a2da-b384b1ff855a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3)$, and\n\n$$\nf'(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\non this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-2) &= 3 \\\\\nf(3) &= f(-2) + \\int_{-2}^{3}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= 3 + (-10) \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=3\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=-2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1069 "e-442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $1 \\leq x \\leq 5$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below. Find a value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2$.\n\n![](images/e4/e4327bc76ae23907fb628cc9e9d5d045ab773070f9ce8fc2972dcf78e434b24d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be6b45d4-9257-5a84-8e19-50a315871d3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5)$, and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=2$ and $x=4$. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,4,5$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7f129ef5fe5d219f1bf57425682cea13ff657b9a539dc7ae0b2d172bf37c2c28.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= -2 \\\\\nf(2) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nf(4) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{4}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{3}{2} - 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nf(5) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + \\frac{3}{2} - 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\left\\lbrace -2,-\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{2},-1 \\right\\rbrace = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1069 "e-443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,6]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,6]$ given that $f(0)=1$.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca8e07d3438890b4f1e220e491d4a20f35c7473a31e76426b9a1fa13ed6926c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fbd4cac-e923-57fd-94f3-79e323195d1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6)$, and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=4$. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4,6$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= 1 \\\\\nf(4) &= f(0) + \\int_{0}^{4}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 \\\\\n&= 7 \\\\\nf(6) &= f(0) + \\int_{0}^{6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 1 + 6 - 2 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{1, 7, 5\\} = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=7\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=4\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100961 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1045 "Maximizing a Function Using the Graph of Its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101079 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "145ac031-2a3a-531e-b695-a527eb78e27d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101160 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/0669cf5e31d559c70c22c5154c0e43638eb5df8a11bedab1e5eea4dc4d7ab90f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 2, 2]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7$. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 2, 2]$ given that $f(-2) = 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b935d32-f672-54cd-918e-5cf82b270c6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3f2d06871f14bead2e13d9fcb373560dcd74b30f74c3d8f9a6319762130ec162.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 3, 3]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 3, 3]$ given that $f(-3) =-6$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da3b0feb-e320-535c-8435-9a7c6b6e71cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1061 1572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2b6e44b-f469-543a-a1ac-99aecca54d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101655 #edb/ref 1061 1573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "adca67a5-ec48-5624-857c-3dfe325add06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1061 1577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "571b8ef9-5e66-53a6-b671-21d8c331aca2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1061 37716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a96fc6c3-1e3a-55cd-a97a-a9adb0305a19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to maximize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x)$, the *derivative* of $f(x)$, is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0f93de1825106880b3fdcd3c704c72e0748df277e5098064d6ee9abc9bdf8.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that\n\n$$\nf(-2)=3\n$$\n\nOur goal is find the *maximum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2]$.\n\nTo find the maximum value of $f(x)$, we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2)$, so there are no non-stationary critical points. Additionally, we see that $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-1$. Therefore, the candidates are\n\n$$\nx=-2,\\quad x=-1,\\quad x=2\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the left endpoint is given:\n$f(-2) = 3$\n- To compute $f(-1)$, we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus. Since the area of the left triangle in our picture equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$, we have\n$f(-1)|= f(-2) + \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{-1}f^{′}(x)dx; = 3 + 1; = 4$.\n\n- To compute $f(2)$, we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus once more. Using the fact that the area of the left triangle equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and the area of the right triangle equals ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac32}}$, we have\n$f(2)|= f(-2) + \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx; = 3 + 1 - \\frac{3}{2}; = \\frac{5}{2}$.\nNotice that we *subtracted* the area of the right triangle since it lies *under* the $x$-axis.\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\left\\lbrace 3, 4, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\rbrace = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=4\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=-1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1065 819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0533716e-c304-5c45-8aa3-f51853f120c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
